Implemented a Track B generator based on the finite-field coefficient identity in Lemma 2.6 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.06906).

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/gen_1701_06906.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1701.06906/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

All local gates pass. Highlights: 0/200,000 random guesses, five attacks at 0/8 successes each, 12.8M-operation reference evaluation, 256-operation intended route, and a 64-element/332-character answer.

The result is not submission-ready: the available OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare and G9 attempt. The transcripts honestly contain only API errors, so no oracle-hardness verdict is claimed. A funded/replacement `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is required to rerun Step 4 and finalize the shipping preset.
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..908ace3214ebbe6a6d7a81488fb747a8c389ca0c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,138 @@
+# Verified generator for arXiv:1701.06906
+
+## Profile
+
+| field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | polynomial identity |
+| Certificate form | polynomial coefficient vector |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+This generator turns the coefficient identity in the proof of Lemma 2.6 of
+[Gavioli–Gül–Scoppola, *Metabelian thin Beauville p-groups*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.06906)
+into an exact problem family. The solver is given selected monomials of the
+native bivariate polynomial
+
+`P(u,v) = sum_(r=0)^(p-1) (1+u)^r (1+v)^r` over `F_p`, followed by invertible
+two-coordinate basis changes. It must return the resulting coefficient vector.
+The checker evaluates the paper's exact characteristic-`p` identity and performs
+the displayed 2 by 2 matrix products; it never reads the planted answer.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+The literal definition gives
+`c(i,j)=sum_r binom(r,i)binom(r,j) mod p`. The strongest implemented mechanical
+reference evaluator uses factorial tables and binomial recurrences in `O(kp)`
+field operations. On the hard shipping instance used by the saved self-test it
+processed 2,053,947 binomial terms and 12,825,889 counted exact operations in
+0.872 seconds; across eight shipping instances it averaged 13,342,769 operations
+and 0.898 seconds.
+
+The compression is the identity used between Lemmas 2.5 and 2.6:
+with `w=(1+u)(1+v)=1+(u+v+uv)`, the full power sum collapses in characteristic
+`p`. At total degree at most `p-1`, only one homogeneous boundary can survive.
+Classifying 64 queries and applying 32 small matrices costs at most 256 exact
+operations. This is an efficient route and is disclosed; the benchmark asks
+whether a no-tool solver can discover it and carry out the fixed-size coordinate
+work. The easy regime avoided here is precisely a small `p`, where direct
+coefficient accumulation is hand-scale. The paper's separate class-`<p`
+Beauville criterion and its GAP classification of thin 3-groups are not used.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+With `make_instance(n=7, k=4, seed=0)`, the prime is 11 and the complete block
+data are:
+
+```text
+0: (1,9) ; (2,8) ; [[6,4],[7,5]]
+1: (1,7) ; (1,1) ; [[9,3],[8,2]]
+```
+
+The required answer is `<answer>[9, 9, 0, 0]</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, [9, 9, 0, 0])` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping the final entry
+returns `(False, "expected exactly 4 output coordinates")`. A person can solve
+this demo on paper either by accumulating ten short binomial terms per query or
+by spotting the identity.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | lower prime scale `n` | coefficients `k` | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 7 | 4 | hand example; never ships |
+| easy | 1,009 | 64 | hardening pending |
+| medium | 10,007 | 64 | hardening pending |
+| hard | 100,003 | 64 | **shipping preset**, subject to oracle evidence |
+
+Only the haystack grows: the direct sum gets longer while the answer remains 64
+coordinates.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses verified and JSON-round-tripped |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged fenced JSON with surrounding prose round-tripped |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 uniform structure-aware guesses |
+| G5 | pass | unique answer; sampled density 0/200,000; 12,825,889-op reference run |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks, 0/8 successes each; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | prime grew from 100,403 to 202,817; answer stayed length 64 |
+| G8 | pass | 20/20 invariant keys, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 distinct instances |
+| G9(c) | pass | 332 characters, 83 estimated tokens, 64 atoms, 256 operations |
+
+The five failed attacks are all-zero source coefficients, all-one source
+coefficients, an unsigned boundary rule, applying parity to every query, and a
+uniform random coefficient vector. Independent `GL(2,p)` matrices remove the
+obvious raw `0,+1,-1` answer signature without changing the polynomial problem.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+| run | preset | solved/attempts | result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 valid attempts | blocked: OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+| structural hint | hard | 0/0 valid attempts | blocked: OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0/0 valid attempts | blocked: OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+Each required harness arm was invoked, but all retries were API errors and
+therefore correctly counted as **no attempts**, not model failures. No hardness
+verdict is claimed until a funded OpenRouter credential reruns the bare,
+structural, and placebo arms. Consequently `hinted - placebo` is not yet a
+meaningful diagnostic. The local size figures above are measured and gated.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import random
+import gen_1701_06906 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(**g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY], seed=42)
+statement = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + str(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+assert g.random_candidate(inst, random.Random(1)) != inst["answer"]
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit after a successful oracle run with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1701.06906 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This family becomes easy for any solver that recognizes the characteristic-`p`
+power-sum identity and has reliable modular arithmetic; that is intentional for
+Track B. The `0/200,000` guess result is only for the declared uniform prior on
+all length-64 vectors over `F_p`; it does not model a solver that has inferred
+the hidden `0,+1,-1` source coefficients. The adversary panel does not include
+a CAS generating-function simplifier, because that is the successful compact
+route rather than a failing attack. It also does not benchmark alternative
+hypergeometric-summation packages. Most importantly, the four-vendor oracle and
+both G9 comparison arms remain unmeasured because the available API key had
+exhausted its total limit; this directory is not submission-ready until those
+runs produce valid transcripts.
